# ============================================================================
# BOOTSTRAP SELF-HOST — `Effect` facade collision: THE TRACED CAUSE
# (2026-08-06, cycle 6). Read this BEFORE the cycle-2/cycle-3 notes below,
# which record a theory that was tested by a real Stage 3 run and DISPROVED.
# ============================================================================
#
# Symptom (3x, Stage 3 / in-process native-build of bootstrap_main.spl):
#   error: HIR lowering error in src/compiler/mir/__init__.spl:
#   enum payload dependency `Effect` conflicts:
#     `compiler.hir.hir_types::Effect::struct`
#     vs `compiler.mir.mir_effects::Effect::enum`
#
# The actual dependency path (traced through
# 20.hir/hir_lowering/_Items/module_lowering.spl, not guessed):
#
#   1. `export use compiler.mir.mir_data.{MirModule, MirFunction, ...}` above
#      (line ~18) is registered by register_imported_symbol(..., materialize_
#      enum = TRUE) — every top-level named import materializes (call site
#      module_lowering.spl:1572).
#   2. `MirFunction`'s terminal declaration is mir_instruction_graph.spl:159,
#      and it has the field `type_bindings: Dict<text, HirType>` (line 173).
#      The composite branch walks every field's named dependencies
#      (module_lowering.spl:753-761), so `HirType` is registered next, with
#      materialize_enum still TRUE.
#   3. `struct HirType` (hir_types.spl:730) has field `kind: HirTypeKind`, so
#      the walk registers `HirTypeKind` — an ENUM — and the enum branch with
#      materialize_enum=true calls register_materialized_enum_payload_
#      dependencies (module_lowering.spl:795-799).
#   4. `enum HirTypeKind` has the variant
#      `Function(params: [HirType], ret: HirType, effects: [Effect])`
#      (hir_types.spl:768). Walking that payload claims the local name
#      `Effect` for `compiler.hir.hir_types::Effect::struct`
#      (hir_types.spl:912) in materialized_payload_bindings.
#   5. The later `export use compiler.mir.mir_effects.{... Effect ...}` then
#      claims the SAME local name for `compiler.mir.mir_effects::Effect::enum`
#      -> claim_materialized_payload_binding mismatch ->
#      self.error(...) at module_lowering.spl:1175.
#
# So the collision is NOT caused by any re-exported FUNCTION. A callable's
# parameter/return types are never materialized during import registration:
# declared_surface_callable_type returns nil while
# `self.registering_import_symbols` is set (module_lowering.spl:430-431). That
# is precisely why the cycle-2 removal (synthetic-driver functions taking
# `fn_: HirFunction`) and the cycle-3 removal (`MirLowering`/`MirError`,
# reached through `lower_function(fn_: HirFunction)`) changed nothing: a full
# Stage 3 replay pinned at commit 9bb8727cbc3 — which contains BOTH removals —
# reproduced the identical error, byte for byte.
#
# `HirType` is load-bearing on `MirFunction`, and `MirFunction` is the whole
# point of this facade (its only real consumer, 70.backend/codegen.spl:15,
# imports it), so the hir-side `Effect` binding cannot be removed. The mir-side
# one can: NOTHING in the tree imports `Effect` or `EffectSet` through this
# facade. The only two `use compiler.mir.{...}` sites in the repo are
# src/compiler/70.backend/codegen.spl:15 and
# test/01_unit/compiler/mir_opt/target_family_package_surface_spec.spl:9, and
# neither names `Effect`/`EffectSet`; every real user imports them from
# `compiler.mir.mir_effects` directly. `EffectSet` has to go too, not just
# `Effect`: its field `effects: [Effect]` (mir_effects.spl:160) would re-enter
# the same materializing walk through the array-element branch
# (module_lowering.spl:757-761) and re-raise the identical error.
#
# This is the `BackendKind`/`CompiledSymbolKind` remedy from the header of
# src/compiler/70.backend/backend_types.spl: collapse the colliding name to ONE
# terminal binding in this lowering scope. Here that terminal binding is
# `compiler.hir.hir_types::Effect` (arriving transitively via MirFunction), and
# the dead mir-side re-export is what gets dropped.
#
# doc/08_tracking/bug/t3_full_bootstrap_stage3_unresolved_type_byteorder_cache_validator_2026-08-06.md

# Re-exported from mir_lowering.spl
#
# BOOTSTRAP SELF-HOST (2026-08-06, cycle 3): SUPERSEDED DIAGNOSIS — kept for
# the record. The removal itself is harmless (these re-exports really do have
# zero callers), but it did NOT fix the Stage 3 failure; see the traced cause
# above. Original reasoning follows.
#
# removed for the same reason as
# the synthetic-driver re-exports below. `compiler.mir.{MirLowering}` /
# `compiler.mir.{MirError}` (i.e. through THIS facade) have zero callers in
# the tree (verified: grep for `compiler.mir.{...MirLowering...}` /
# `...MirError...` outside src/compiler/50.mir/ itself is empty); the one
# real caller, src/compiler/80.driver/pipeline_fn.spl:13, already imports
# `use compiler.mir.mir_lowering.{MirLowering}` directly. `MirLowering` is
# re-exported (wildcard, via mir_lowering.spl -> compiler.mir._MirLowering.
# function_lowering.*) from `_MirLowering/function_lowering.spl`, whose
# core entry point `me lower_function(fn_: HirFunction) -> MirFunction`
# (function_lowering.spl:69) is the SAME `Effect`-materializing dependency
# as the synthetic-driver functions below, just load-bearing rather than
# dead code -- so unlike the synthetic-driver case this one cannot be fixed
# by finding an unused re-export; it genuinely needs `HirFunction` (MIR
# lowering's whole job is consuming HIR functions). Removing the re-export
# here (cycle 2 fix, synthetic-driver only) was therefore insufficient on
# its own: Stage 3 hit the identical `Effect` conflict again at the same
# file/line on retry. This is the real fix: keep `MirLowering`/`MirError`
# reachable only via the direct submodule path, which is what every actual
# caller already used.
# doc/08_tracking/bug/t3_full_bootstrap_stage3_unresolved_type_byteorder_cache_validator_2026-08-06.md

# FR-DRIVER-0001 static-registration synthesis planner + codegen
#
# BOOTSTRAP SELF-HOST (2026-08-06, cycle 2): SUPERSEDED DIAGNOSIS — kept for
# the record. The dead-code claim is correct; the `Effect`-materialization
# claim is NOT (a re-exported function's parameter types are never
# materialized during import registration). A Stage 3 replay pinned at the
# commit carrying this removal reproduced the identical error. See the traced
# cause near the top of this section. Original reasoning follows.
#
# these two re-exports used to live here,
# but every real caller (src/compiler/50.mir/_MirLowering/{asm_and_targets,
# module_lowering,function_lowering,bootstrap_globals}.spl) already imports
# `apply_synthetic_driver_codegen` directly from
# `compiler.mir.synthetic_driver_codegen`, never through this facade; nothing
# in the tree imports `plan_synthetic_driver_registration` /
# `SyntheticDriverRegistrationStatus` / `SyntheticDriverRegistrationPlan`
# through `compiler.mir` either (verified: zero grep hits for
# `compiler.mir.{...SyntheticDriverRegistration...}` outside the two
# declaring files). So these re-exports were dead weight -- but not harmless
# dead weight: both re-exported functions take `fn_: HirFunction`
# (compiler.hir.hir_definitions), whose `effects: [Effect]` field
# (hir_definitions.spl:34) transitively materializes `Effect` as
# `compiler.hir.hir_types::Effect::struct` in THIS file's lowering scope --
# colliding with the `Effect` enum this file already exports explicitly from
# `compiler.mir.mir_effects` two blocks up. The self-hosted HIR resolver hard-
# errors on that same-name divergent-kind collision (self.error("enum payload
# dependency `Effect` conflicts: ...") in
# 20.hir/hir_lowering/_Items/module_lowering.spl:1175); the Rust seed doesn't
# surface it. Same defect class as the `BackendKind`/`CompiledSymbolKind`
# facade collision documented at the top of
# src/compiler/70.backend/backend_types.spl -- fixed the same way, by
# collapsing to one terminal binding for the colliding name, here by dropping
# the dead re-export rather than renaming a type.
# doc/08_tracking/bug/t3_full_bootstrap_stage3_unresolved_type_byteorder_cache_validator_2026-08-06.md
